{"name":"Pigeon Post","hex":"#afbdd9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pigeon post","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The use of homing pigeons to carry messages.","The university's internal mail system."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pigeon_post"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pigeon Post","blocks":[{"text":"Pigeon Post is an English children's adventure novel by Arthur Ransome, published by Jonathan Cape in 1936. It was the sixth of twelve books Ransome completed in the Swallows and Amazons series. He won the inaugural Carnegie Medal from the Library Association, recognising it as the year's best children's book by a British subject."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Pigeon_Post_cover.jpg/330px-Pigeon_Post_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pigeon_Post","wikidata":"Q7193316"},{"title":"Pigeon post","blocks":[{"text":"Pigeon post is the use of homing pigeons to carry messages. Pigeons are effective as messengers due to their natural homing abilities. The pigeons are transported to a destination in cages, where they are attached with messages, then the pigeon naturally flies back to its home where the recipient could read the message. They have been used in many places around the world. Pigeons have also been used to great effect in military situations, and are in this case referred to as war pigeons."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Pigeon_Messengers_%28Harper%27s_Engraving%29.png/330px-Pigeon_Messengers_%28Harper%27s_Engraving%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pigeon_post","wikidata":"Q2142909"},{"title":"Columbidae","blocks":[{"text":"Columbidae is a bird family consisting of doves and pigeons. It is the only family in the order Columbiformes. These are stout-bodied birds with small heads, relatively short necks and slender bills that in some species feature fleshy ceres. They feed largely on plant matter, feeding on seeds (granivory), fruit (frugivory), and foliage (folivory)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Treron_vernans_male_-_Kent_Ridge_Park.jpg/330px-Treron_vernans_male_-_Kent_Ridge_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbidae","wikidata":"Q10856"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Junge_Frau_mit_Taubenpost.jpg/500px-Junge_Frau_mit_Taubenpost.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Junge_Frau_mit_Taubenpost.jpg/500px-Junge_Frau_mit_Taubenpost.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Young lady in oriental clothing with a homing pigeon (19th century painting)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJunge_Frau_mit_Taubenpost.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Barabas-galambposta.jpg/500px-Barabas-galambposta.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Barabas-galambposta.jpg/500px-Barabas-galambposta.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pigeon post (1843 painting by Miklós Barabás)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABarabas-galambposta.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Siege_of_Paris_1870-1871%2C_Pigeon_Post_Medal_for_French_Military_Communications.jpg/500px-Siege_of_Paris_1870-1871%2C_Pigeon_Post_Medal_for_French_Military_Communications.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Siege_of_Paris_1870-1871%2C_Pigeon_Post_Med